'''Companyia Química Occidental, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Companyia Química Occidental}}), commonly known as '''CQO''', is a Balboan diversified chemicals company and one of the country's largest chemical manufacturers. Formed in 1954 from the post-war merger of [[Papoa Siokte Huàxué]] and [[Corporació Química Unida de Balboa]], the company produces a range of commodity and specialty chemicals, including {{w|polycrystalline silicon}}, {{w|hydrogen peroxide}}, {{w|fumed silica}}, {{w|coal tar pitch}}, {{w|BTX (chemistry)|BTX}}, and {{w|phosphoric acid}}. Headquartered in the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] of [[Sant Cristòfor]], CQO operates its main production complex at [[Badia Curta]] in [[Jala]] province, adjacent to [[CIEA]]'s refinery. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: CQO) and is a constituent of the [[SCX 30]] index.

CQO occupies a strategically important position in two global supply chains. In the {{w|semiconductor}} industry, the company is a major supplier of hyper-pure electronic-grade {{w|polysilicon}}, {{w|hydrogen peroxide}}, {{w|fumed silica}}, and {{w|phosphoric acid}} to wafer and chip manufacturers in [[Okaiken]], [[Balisca]], and [[Breisland]]. In the {{w|electric vehicle|EV}} battery industry, CQO supplies {{w|coal tar pitch|high softening point pitch}} (HSPP) for {{w|anode|anode materials}} to battery manufacturers in [[Louyang]], [[Breisland]], and the [[United Midlands]]. Together with [[Grup Químic Central]] (GQC), which produces fluorochemicals for semiconductor fabrication, CQO gives Balboa significant leverage over the semiconductor supply chains of Okaiken and Balisca.

== History ==

=== Predecessors ===

==== Papoa Siokte Huàxué (1928–1954) ====

Papoa Siokte Huàxué was founded in 1928 by Lîm Hui-sheng, a Zong-Balboan chemical engineer who had emigrated from [[Sinchew]] to [[Portnou]] during the [[Zong Balboa]] era. The company's name combined the Zong term for the Balboan isthmus region with the Sinchewese word for chemistry ({{lang|zh|化學}}). Lîm established the company's first facility in Portnou as a general chemistry company, producing basic industrial chemicals — {{w|coal tar}} distillation products, {{w|sulfuric acid}}, and {{w|alkali}} chemicals — for the domestic market of the Zong Balboa territory.

The company grew modestly during the prosperous "Shining Star" era of the late 1920s and early 1930s, supplying chemicals to the construction, textile, and metalworking industries of the canal corridor. During the [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947), the Portnou facility was repurposed for wartime production, manufacturing explosives precursors and chemical reagents. The facility survived the war despite the widespread devastation of the region, and resumed commercial production after the cessation of hostilities in 1947.

==== Corporació Química Unida de Balboa (1934–1954) ====

Corporació Química Unida de Balboa was founded in 1934 by Francesc Bosch i Vidal, a Volisanian-Balboan chemist, on the eve of the [[Great Adonian War]]. Bosch established CQUB to produce specialty chemicals — primarily {{w|phosphoric acid}} and {{w|hydrogen peroxide}} — for the domestic market. The company operated a small plant in the Sant Cristòfor industrial district.

During the war, CQUB's facilities were damaged in the siege of Sant Cristòfor, but Bosch maintained limited production throughout, supplying chemicals for civil defence and medical applications. After the war, Bosch rebuilt the business with backing from the [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB), which had been established to support post-war reconstruction of the Balboan industrial base.

=== Merger (1954) ===

In 1954, during the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|Adonian Community mandate]] period, Papoa Siokte Huàxué and Corporació Química Unida de Balboa merged to form Companyia Química Occidental. The merger was driven by the complementary strengths of the two companies — Papoa Siokte Huàxué's coal tar distillation and basic chemicals capacity, and CQUB's specialty chemicals and phosphoric acid production — and by the broader post-war imperative to consolidate the Balboan chemical industry under the AC administration. The combined entity adopted the name "Companyia Química Occidental" — "Occidental Chemical Company" — to reflect its Balboan identity and its orientation as a western supplier to global markets.

The merger was structured as a stock-for-stock transaction, with shareholders of both predecessor companies receiving shares in the new entity. The Lîm family (from Papoa Siokte Huàxué) retained a significant stake, while the [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB) maintained its strategic holding. Lîm Hui-sheng, the founder of Papoa Siokte Huàxué, became the first chairman of the merged company, while Francesc Bosch i Vidal served as technical director.

=== Post-merger growth (1954–present) ===

Following the merger, CQO continued to operate from the predecessor companies' existing facilities in Portnou and Sant Cristòfor. Through the late 1950s and early 1960s, the company focused on basic and commodity chemicals, supplying coal tar pitch, BTX, sulfuric acid, phosphoric acid, and hydrogen peroxide to the domestic market of the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|Adonian Community mandate]] and, after 1960, to the newly reunified Balboan economy.

In 1964, four years after the mandate was returned to Balboa, CQO relocated its production operations to the [[Badia Curta]] industrial complex in [[Jala]] province. Badia Curta had been developed in the early 1950s by the Balboan rump state as an industrial centre outside the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|Adonian Community mandate]] area, and was the site of [[CIEA]]'s refinery, built by Petrolí de Balboa in the early 1950s. The move to Badia Curta consolidated the company's scattered facilities into a single site and provided access to larger plots, deep-water port access, and co-location with CIEA's refinery, which would later prove critical for the company's polysilicon and hydrogen peroxide operations.

In the 1970s, following the return of the mandate to Balboa in 1960 and the subsequent economic expansion, CQO began investing in higher-value products. The company entered into a long-term hydrogen supply agreement with [[CIEA]], whose refinery at Badia Curta provided a low-cost source of hydrogen. In the 1980s, CQO invested in {{w|polycrystalline silicon}} production using the {{w|Siemens process}}, establishing a polysilicon plant at Badia Curta with hydrogen feedstock supplied from CIEA's refinery. The company also began producing {{w|fumed silica}} and electronic-grade {{w|phosphoric acid}} for the semiconductor industry. By the 1990s, CQO had become a supplier to semiconductor manufacturers in [[Okaiken]] and [[Balisca]].

CQO was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1965, following the exchange's reopening under BdSC Corporació.

In the 2010s, CQO entered the EV battery materials market, developing {{w|coal tar pitch|high softening point pitch}} (HSPP) from its coal tar distillation operations. HSPP is a critical component in the manufacture of {{w|lithium-ion battery|lithium-ion battery}} anodes, and CQO's existing coal tar pitch expertise gave it a natural advantage. The company began exporting HSPP to battery manufacturers in [[Louyang]], [[Breisland]], and the [[United Midlands]], capitalising on the rapid growth of the EV industry.

CQO's exports benefit from the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]], which offsets the overvalued ID's cost penalty on manufactured goods exports. The company's principal export markets are [[Okaiken]] and [[Balisca]] (semiconductor materials), and [[Louyang]], [[Breisland]], and the [[United Midlands]] (battery materials and basic chemicals).

=== Current leadership ===

The current chairman is [[Lîm Wei-jun]], grandson of founder Lîm Hui-sheng, who has served on the board since 2005 and as chairman since 2012. The chief executive officer is [[Jericho Warag]], a Burawi-Balboan chemical industry executive who joined CQO in 2018 after leading the company's polysilicon division and was appointed CEO in 2022. Warag is the first non-Lîm-family CEO in the company's history and holds no family ownership stake.

== Products ==

CQO's product portfolio spans three main categories:

=== Semiconductor materials ===

* '''Polycrystalline silicon (polysilicon)''': Hyper-pure electronic-grade polysilicon produced via the {{w|Siemens process}} for semiconductor wafer manufacturing. CQO's polysilicon is supplied to wafer manufacturers in [[Okaiken]] and [[Balisca]]. Hydrogen feedstock is supplied from [[CIEA]]'s refinery via direct pipeline.
* '''Hydrogen peroxide''': Semiconductor-grade {{w|hydrogen peroxide}} for wafer cleaning and surface preparation. Also produced in industrial grades for bleaching and chemical synthesis.
* '''Fumed silica''': High-purity {{w|fumed silica}} used in {{w|chemical mechanical planarization|CMP slurries}} for semiconductor fabrication, as well as in adhesives, coatings, and battery separators.
* '''Phosphoric acid''': Electronic-grade {{w|phosphoric acid}} used as a {{w|wet etching|wet etchant}} for silicon nitride layers in semiconductor fabrication. Also produced in technical and food grades.

=== Battery materials ===

* '''High softening point pitch (HSPP)''': A specialised grade of {{w|coal tar pitch}} with a softening point above 250°C, used as a {{w|pitch (material)|binder and impregnating agent}} in the manufacture of {{w|lithium-ion battery|lithium-ion battery}} anodes. CQO produces HSPP from coal tar distillation, leveraging its existing coal tar pitch operations. HSPP is exported to EV battery manufacturers in [[Louyang]], [[Breisland]], and the [[United Midlands]].

=== Basic chemicals ===

* '''Coal tar pitch''': Produced from coal tar distillation for use in aluminium smelting (anode production) and as a feedstock for HSPP production. CQO is the largest domestic producer of coal tar pitch in Balboa.
* '''BTX (benzene, toluene, xylene)''': Produced from coal tar distillation as a byproduct of coal tar pitch production. CQO's BTX is sold as chemical feedstock for synthesis applications, complementing — but not competing directly with — [[Corporació Nacional de Fabricants de Solvents]] (CNFS), which produces BTX from petroleum refining for solvent applications.
* '''Hydrogen peroxide (industrial grade)''': Industrial-grade hydrogen peroxide for bleaching, water treatment, and chemical synthesis.

== Operations ==

* '''Headquarters''': [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]], [[Sant Cristòfor]]
* '''Main production complex''': [[Badia Curta]], [[Jala]] province — co-located with [[CIEA]]'s refinery, with direct pipeline access to hydrogen and other refinery feedstocks
* '''Polysilicon plant''': [[Badia Curta]] — Siemens process reactors, with hydrogen supplied from CIEA
* '''Coal tar distillation''': [[Badia Curta]] — processes coal tar from coke ovens in the interior into coal tar pitch, BTX, and HSPP
* '''Export terminal''': [[Port de Portnou]] — handles export shipments to Okaiken, Balisca, Louyang, Breisland, and the United Midlands
* '''Feedstocks''': Hydrogen from CIEA refinery; coal tar from domestic coke ovens; metallurgical-grade silicon from imports
* '''Employees''': approximately 9,000 (2026)

The Badia Curta complex is CQO's primary production site, housing polysilicon reactors, coal tar distillation columns, hydrogen peroxide plants, fumed silica reactors, and phosphoric acid production. The site's co-location with CIEA's refinery provides a significant cost advantage for hydrogen-dependent processes, particularly polysilicon production via the Siemens process.

== Ownership ==

CQO is publicly traded on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]]. The ownership structure reflects the 1954 merger:

* '''Lîm family''': 12% — descendants of Lîm Hui-sheng, founder of Papoa Siokte Huàxué; represented by chairman Lîm Wei-jun
* '''[[CIEA]]''': 5% — strategic stake tied to the long-term hydrogen supply agreement
* '''[[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB)''': 5% — strategic stake dating from post-war reconstruction of CQUB
* '''Public float''': approximately 78% — held by institutional and retail investors

The Lîm family controls 12% of shares, providing continuity of the founding family's influence while maintaining a broadly held public market for the majority of the stock.

